      <p>Amla (Phyllanthus emblica) is a round, pale-green fruit with a bright, sour taste. It's eaten fresh, pickled, dried and powdered across India.</p><p>Amla is a natural source of Vitamin C — our label lists 25 mg per serving.</p>
      <h2 id="highlights">Highlights</h2><ul class="ba-hl"><li>Botanical name: Phyllanthus emblica. Part used: fruit.</li><li>Taste: very sour, tangy and refreshing.</li><li>Natural source of Vitamin C (28% DV per serving on our label).</li></ul>
      <h2 id="s0">In the kitchen</h2><p>Because Amla is so sour, people often mix it into juices, citrus tonics or smoothies rather than water alone.</p><h2 id="s1">One of Triphala's three fruits</h2><p>Amla (also called Amalaki) is one of the three fruits that make up Triphala.</p>
      
